'''''Pulicaria dysenterica''''', the '''common fleabane''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17 }}</ref> or, in North America, '''meadow false fleabane''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=PUDY|taxon=Pulicaria dysenterica|accessdate=16 October 2015}}</ref> is a species of [[fleabane]] in the family [[Asteraceae]].  It is native to Europe and western Asia where it grows in a variety of habitats ranging from semi-arid [[Mediterranean forests, woodlands, and scrub|Mediterranean woodlands]] to wetter situations.<ref name="chestofbooks.com">{{cite web|url=http://chestofbooks.com/flora-plants/flowers/British-Wild-Flowers-1/Fleabane-Pulicaria-Dysenterica-Gray.html|title=Fleabane (Pulicaria Dysenterica, Gray)}}</ref> ''Pulicaria dysenterica'' is perennial and can form dense clusters of plants, spreading by its roots. It flowers at its maximum height of about {{convert|60|cm|ft}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.pfaf.org/database/plants.php?Pulicaria+dysenterica|title = Pulicaria dysenterica Fleabane, Meadow false fleabane PFAF Plant Database}}</ref> Leaves are alternately arranged and clasp the stem, which itself contains a salty-[[astringent]] liquid. The yellow [[head (botany)|inflorescences]] are typically composed of a prominent centre of 40–100 [[disc flower|disc florets]] surrounded by 20–30 narrow, pistillate [[ray flower|ray florets]]. When setting seed the flower heads reflex.<ref name="FNA">{{cite web|url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=127631 |title=Pulicaria in Flora of North America @ |publisher=Efloras.org |date= |accessdate=2022-05-01}}</ref>

Common fleabane is the main food plant for the fleabane tortoise beetle (''[[Cassida murraea]]''),<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.coleoptera.org.uk/species/cassida-murraea|title = Cassida murraea Linnaeus, 1767 &#124; UK Beetle Recording}}</ref> and for four micromoths, ''[[Apodia bifractella]]'',<ref>{{cite web|url=https://ukmoths.org.uk/species/apodia-bifractella/|title = Apodia bifractella - UKMoths}}</ref> ''[[Ptocheuusa paupella]]'',<ref>{{cite web|url=https://ukmoths.org.uk/species/ptocheuusa-paupella/|title=Ptocheuusa paupella - UKMoths}}</ref> dusky plume (''[[Oidaematophorus lithodactyla]]'')<ref>{{cite web|url=https://ukmoths.org.uk/species/oidaematophorus-lithodactyla/|title=Dusky Plume Oidaematophorus lithodactyla - UKMoths}}</ref> and ''[[Digitivalva pulicariae]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://ukmoths.org.uk/species/digitivalva-pulicariae/|title=Digitivalva pulicariae - UKMoths}}</ref>

Fleabane's common name comes from its former use as an incense to drive away insects.<ref name="FNA"/> Other past uses include treatments for [[dysentery]] and unspecified ocular maladies.<ref name="chestofbooks.com"/>

